pr.body:
Closes #11 (second of two PRs; #20 covered the verdict, loop states, round limit and new-commit handling).

Before: Orbit's verdict and loop state were only visible inside Orbit. An author agent or someone on the PR page couldn't tell whether the latest round was clean.

After: while automatic publication is on, each PR's latest automatic round sets the **orbit/review** commit status on its reviewed commit:
- `pending` while the round is in progress;
- `success` when clean;
- `failure` when changes are requested;
- `error` when the loop needs you.

The description is short, e.g. "Round 2 · 1 blocking finding". Published comments also carry a hidden line such as `<!-- orbit-verdict: changes_requested round=2 blocking=1 -->`. A new **GitHub status check** setting (on by default) turns the statuses off.

## How

- `review_loop::wanted_commit_checks` works out at most one wanted status per commit:
  - for each PR's current head, from its latest round's loop state (`wanted_status`);
  - for any check Orbit left `pending` that no current round owns (stopped, replaced, or finished just before a push), a final status from the newest automatic run on that commit (`closing_status`).
- `round_number` numbers automatic rounds for the comment marker.
- `engine/status.rs`: `sync_commit_statuses` runs on every tick (`Engine::tick`), including while Codex is disconnected. It sends a status only when it differs from the one recorded for that commit in `Snapshot.commit_checks`, one request per `repo@sha` at a time, and recomputes after each, so the newest round wins. If GitHub refuses a status, the refusal is recorded and shown in the PR detail's loop panel. It isn't retried until the wanted status changes, and reviews are unaffected.
- `github::set_status` posts to `repos/{repo}/statuses/{sha}` with context `orbit/review`. The verdict marker goes on the line after the existing `orbit-review` marker.
- The status sender can be replaced in tests, and its test default never calls GitHub.

## Decisions

- Statuses need both **GitHub status check** and **Automatic publication** on. I read "on by default whenever automatic publication is on" as: nothing is sent to GitHub while you are still reviewing reports by hand. Checks Orbit already opened are still closed after you turn it off.
- A stopped loop keeps the round's verdict (success or failure) rather than showing an error.
- Orbit never configures branch protection (out of scope, as the issue says).

## Checks

`npm run check` passes locally (70 frontend tests, 154 Rust tests, clippy, desktop compile). There are new tests for:
- each status state;
- the settings gates;
- round numbering;
- the 140-character limit;
- closing stale pending checks;
- sending each status once;
- refusals;
- the comment marker.

There are also regression tests for the three review findings: a slow stale write, a Codex disconnect, and a round finishing before a push.

